To improve logging, add a new logging function in 'src/utils/logger.js'. Here’s an example:

```javascript
function logVulnerability(vulnerability) {
    console.log(`Vulnerability found: ${vulnerability.id}`);
    console.log(`Severity: ${vulnerability.severity}`);
    console.log(`Package: ${vulnerability.package}`);
}
```
Then, call this function in 'src/scanner.js' where you detect vulnerabilities.

First, install Jest if you haven’t done so:
```bash
npm install --save-dev jest
```
Next, add a test in 'tests/scanner.test.js':
```javascript
const { logVulnerability } = require('../src/utils/logger');
describe('Logger Tests', () => {
    it('should log vulnerability details', () => {
        console.log = jest.fn(); // Mock console.log
        logVulnerability({ id: 'CVE-2023-1234', severity: 'high', package: 'example-package' });
        expect(console.log).toHaveBeenCalled();
    });
});
```

The vulnerability data structure can be an object with the following fields:
```javascript
{
    id: 'CVE-2023-1234',
    severity: 'high',
    package: 'example-package',
    description: 'A critical vulnerability',
    url: 'https://vuln.example.com'
}
``` Ensure all relevant details are captured to aid in remediation.

To optimize performance, consider using asynchronous logging. Modify your logging function in 'src/utils/logger.js':
```javascript
async function logVulnerability(vulnerability) {
    await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 10)); // Simulating async operation
    console.log(`Vulnerability found: ${vulnerability.id}`);
}
```
Use async/await syntax when calling logging functions in your scanner.
